  "reasoning": "This concerns the 2026 Florida Republican gubernatorial primary, in which Rep. Byron Donalds was an early frontrunner, boosted by Donald Trump's endorsement (announced in early 2025). Trump-endorsed candidates in open Florida GOP gubernatorial primaries have historically performed very well—e.g., 2018, Trump-endorsed Rep. Ron DeSantis won ~56% against Agriculture Commissioner Adam Putnam despite minor candidates on the ballot. The structural analogy (Trump-endorsed sitting congressman vs. an establishment/statewide-official rival, with a few minor candidates) suggests Donalds could clear 50%. Additionally, Trump's early endorsement may have deterred major rivals (e.g., Casey DeSantis), thinning the field; a near-unopposed race would yield well above 50%. Countervailing risks: multiple candidates splitting the vote, a DeSantis-aligned rival (e.g., the attorney general) consolidating opposition and holding Donalds to a winning-but-sub-50% plurality, Trump wavering on the endorsement, or late surges. Florida requires only a plurality, so dominant frontrunners sometimes finish in the mid-40s in multi-candidate fields. Weighing the strong endorsement, early polling dominance, and the 2018 precedent against field-fragmentation risk, I estimate roughly a 65% chance Donalds reaches 50.000% or more."
}

